W = 8, domain 0..256 exhaustive, limit 255

DEGENERACY 1 IS THE CASE WITH NOTHING TO DECIDE
  wrap, affine, D=3                  depth 3  degeneracy 1
  wrap, affine, D=5                  depth 5  degeneracy 1
  clamp, non-negative additions, D=3 depth 3  degeneracy 1

DEGENERACY ABOVE 1 IS THE CASE THE DESIGN MUST DECIDE
  clamp, mixed, D=3                  depth 3  degeneracy 3
  round to 2^3, affine, D=3          depth 3  degeneracy 3
  round to 2^3, affine, D=5          depth 5  degeneracy 7
  -> rounding's decision space grows with depth: 3 at D=3, 7 at D=5.

WHICH PLACEMENT IS CLOSEST TO THE EXACT COMPOSITE (rounding, D=5)
  fully eager  (mask 0b1111): total |err| 7936, worst |err| 64
  fully defer  (mask 0b0000): total |err| 512, worst |err| 4
  best of all 16 placements: mask 0b0000, total |err| 512

IS DEFERRAL POINTWISE OPTIMAL, OR ONLY IN AGGREGATE?
  The reason to expect it is: every placement ends with the SAME boundary
  resolution, so every output is a representable point; and where that
  resolution is a NEAREST-point projection, the deferred form outputs the
  nearest representable point to the exact value by definition. Nothing
  else can be strictly closer. Below, that is checked rather than argued,
  and then broken on a resolution that is not nearest-point.

  nearest (round to 2^3)   3000 chains searched, 0 with any eager win, 0 winning inputs
  nearest (clamp)          3000 chains searched, 0 with any eager win, 0 winning inputs
  NOT nearest (truncate)   3000 chains searched, 91 with any eager win, 1330 winning inputs
  e.g. [SatSubK(127), AddK(97), MulK(3), XorK(182)] at x=134: eager |err| 2 < deferred |err| 6

RESULT: degeneracy separates the chains that pose a decision from those that
do not. Where it is above one, deferring every interior resolution is the
aggregate and worst-case optimum, and it is also the POINTWISE optimum for
every chain searched whose boundary resolution is a nearest-point projection.
That last part fails as soon as the boundary resolution is not nearest-point,
which is what the truncation row shows, so it is a property of the resolution
rather than of chains in general.
